        "\n",
        "# Biostatistics with Python\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning Statistics Through Cardiovascular Data\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the UCI Cleveland Heart Disease dataset, this module introduces practical statistical reasoning for biomedical data analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "We will move from raw clinical measurements to visual summaries, statistical comparisons, and interpretable models.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Core question\"}\n",
        "How can we use statistics to turn patient-level cardiovascular data into evidence?\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Module Overview\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.grid}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.g-col-4}\n",
        "### Part I  \n",
        "## Descriptive Statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "Explore the dataset, summarise patient variables, and visualise distributions.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.g-col-4}\n",
        "### Part II  \n",
        "## Hypothesis Testing\n",
        "\n",
        "Compare patient groups and decide whether observed differences are likely to reflect meaningful effects.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.g-col-4}\n",
        "### Part III  \n",
        "## Correlation and Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Study relationships between clinical variables and learn how simple models support interpretation.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Dataset at a Glance\n",
        "\n",
        "The module uses the **processed Cleveland database** from the UCI Heart Disease dataset. The UCI repository describes the Heart Disease dataset as containing four databases, with Cleveland being the processed database most commonly used in machine learning studies."
